  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 AM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nNortheast winds early this morning are pushing mid to upper 50\ndewpoints across the Quad State. The NBM high temperatures are \nin the upper 80s to lower 90s. Am somewhat concerned that with \nthe exceptionally dry air overspreading the region, temperatures\nmay over-perform by a few degrees. At least the humidity will \nbe down near 30%, or possibly even lower.\n\nLow-level moisture will spread northwest across the Quad State\nearly Tuesday and south winds will lead to a noticeable increase\nin heat and humidity. Highs on Tuesday will reach the low to mid\n90s and a few locations may approach a heat index of 100.\nWednesday still looks like the hottest day of the week, with\nhighs roughly 95-100, and heat indices 100-104. \n\nThe 00Z GEFS has a 50-80% chance of temperatures reaching the \ncentury mark over virtually all of southeast Missouri and \nsouthern Illinois Wednesday. The GFS has been on the extreme\nside of late, but those probabilities do bear out the potential\nfor some locations to touch 100 once again on Wednesday.\n\nThe consistent signal for showers and thunderstorms to spread \nsouth across the Quad State Wednesday night into Thursday\ncontinues. PoPs are now around 80% over much of the region, with\n50-60% chances from the Bootheel through the southern half of\nwest Kentucky. QPF will be greatest over the northern half of\nthe region where around a third to a half an inch is expected. \nOver the southern half of the region, a quarter inch or less is \nexpected. These QPF trends are reasonably in line with the 00Z \nGEFS. With westerly flow aloft increasing and what should be \nsome at least moderate instability available ahead of the front,\na few severe storms with damaging winds cannot be ruled out \nalong the I-64 corridor Wednesday night.\n\nConsiderable uncertainty exists in the placement of the upper\nridge heading into next weekend. There are some signs of it\npopping back up over the Quad State by Sunday which would mean\ndrier and hotter conditions. However, if it does not amplify\nweak zonal flow will bring chances of convection each day, and\nless hot, but still above normal temperatures.",
